  "title": "Supreme Court Clears Way for Big Bend Border Construction",
  "deck": "An 8–1 order lets the administration resume border infrastructure work in West Texas while the legal fight continues.",
  "body": [
    "The U.S. Supreme Court on November 9 cleared the way for the Trump administration to resume border-barrier construction across the Big Bend region of West Texas, including work affecting Big Bend National Park. In an unsigned 8–1 order, the justices stayed an injunction issued by U.S. District Judge Kathleen Cardone while the legal challenge continues.",
    "The ruling permits work to proceed for now. It is not a final judgment approving every proposed barrier, road or waiver. Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson was the sole publicly dissenting justice.",
    "WHY BIG BEND IS DIFFERENT — Big Bend National Park protects Rio Grande canyons, desert mountains and fragile habitats. Plans for the national park include a noncontiguous vehicle-barrier system, roads and surveillance infrastructure rather than the tall pedestrian walls planned for some other border areas. The administration says gaps in border infrastructure can invite illegal crossings and cartel activity.",
    "The proposal has drawn criticism from conservationists, local business owners, landowners and officials across party lines. Opponents argue that construction could damage wildlife habitat, recreation, historic places and the tourism economy in a sector with comparatively few migrant apprehensions.",
    "WHAT THE JUSTICES DECIDED — The plaintiffs challenged Homeland Security waivers intended to speed construction without complying with certain environmental and historic-preservation laws. Judge Cardone had blocked construction pending further litigation. The Supreme Court concluded that the administration was highly likely to prevail on its argument that federal law restricts lower-court review of the challenged waivers, and it stayed the injunction.",
    "The court weighed the federal government's stated interest in border security against alleged environmental, recreational and aesthetic harms. The stay allows the government to move ahead while the underlying dispute proceeds; it does not establish that construction has already resumed at any particular location.",
    "WHO IS AFFECTED — A Big Bend river guide, an environmental organization and supporters of a historic church in Ruidosa are among the challengers. Local residents and landowners have raised questions about access, property rights and the effect of construction on businesses dependent on the river and surrounding landscape.",
    "THE CENTRAL TRADE-OFF — Supporters of the project argue that border enforcement should not be delayed by extended litigation. Opponents warn that construction could permanently alter protected landscapes before the courts finish reviewing the government's legal authority. Both the government's security objectives and the specific environmental and property impacts deserve scrutiny.",
    "EP PERSPECTIVE — Analysis: The decision changes the immediate balance of power between the administration and its challengers. The government can act now, while the challengers must continue litigating as physical work may proceed. The unanswered questions are where crews will restart, which infrastructure will actually be built, whether safeguards can limit damage, and how the courts will resolve the waiver dispute. Does border security in Big Bend require these particular barriers and roads—or could less intrusive methods achieve the same goals?"
  ],
